  <h2 id="ai">AI and the sector</h2>

  <p>
    At sector level the pressure is clear. Nasscom estimated AI revenue at USD 10 to 12 billion in FY26 and said more
    than 20 lakh of the 59.5 lakh employees were upskilled in AI. A news report of the Infosys annual report says work
    is shifting from writing code toward orchestration, supervision and checking outcomes. A Swarajya report in July
    2026 said nearly two in three new GCC roles needed AI, data or automation skills, though it did not name the study
    behind that share, so treat it as a direction.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What AI is squeezing</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Routine coding and simple scripts.</li>
        <li>Manual test execution and first-line support.</li>
        <li>Templated reports and documents.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What it rewards</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Knowing a domain well enough to catch wrong answers.</li>
        <li>Designing how work flows, with AI inside it.</li>
        <li>Explaining results and risks to clients.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    A staged way to use AI: first, use it daily for your own drafts and debugging and always check the output. Next,
    build one workflow in your lane that saves real time. Later, offer that workflow to a team or a small client.
    Tool familiarity alone is not proof. A result with a clear before and after is.
  </p>

  <p>
    The lane-by-lane breakdown is in
    <a href="/blog/ai-future/ai-impact-on-it-jobs-india/">AI impact on IT jobs in India</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="work-life">Stress, hours and work style</h2>

  <p>
    Stress depends on the lane more than on the sector. Support, operations and security monitoring can mean shifts,
    on-call duty and alert fatigue. Services delivery can mean client time zones and tight release dates. Product
    teams bring deadline pressure and sharper performance reviews. Freelance work removes a boss but adds irregular
    income.
  </p>

  <p>
    Remote work exists in IT, but it varies by employer and role, and some large firms have asked people back to the
    office. Check each employer's current policy before you plan around working from home.
  </p>

  <h2 id="money-learning">Money and learning without waste</h2>

  <p>
    Most IT work does not need a licence, so you do not need to overspend on a degree. A strict starting rule: keep
    roughly 10% of the family education budget for the college fee. If the whole budget is Rs 10 lakh, that is about
    Rs 1 lakh, and the rest pays for a laptop, internet, tools, projects and later courses. Going well above 20%
    needs course-specific proof such as verified placements for that branch, and education loans need extra caution.
  </p>

  <p>
    A degree still has a place: many employers filter on it, and it keeps doors open. The problem is degree-only
    thinking with no skills built beside it.
  </p>

  <p>
    Start free: good YouTube channels, official documentation and free practice sites cover most basics. Pay for a
    course only when you can name what you are buying, such as expert feedback, a structured path or a real project
    review. Check who teaches it, when it was last updated, and whether past learners built something real. A
    pile of certificates is not a skill portfolio.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sales-layer">Who pays, and how they trust you</h2>

  <p>
    In a salaried IT job, your employer pays and the hiring route is an interview, so the sales layer matters mainly
    for the side route and for your next move. For freelance or small consulting work, the payer is a business owner,
    startup or agency with a problem and no time.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Positioning:</strong> say who you help and with what, in one sentence. "I automate monthly reports for small finance teams" beats "I do IT".</li>
    <li><strong>Proof:</strong> one live demo, one small case note, one referral they can check.</li>
    <li><strong>First skill to learn:</strong> a simple offer and a calm price conversation. Ask what the problem costs them before you quote.</li>
    <li><strong>One number to watch:</strong> how many conversations turn into paid trial work.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Also build communication in English and a small network. Both matter in client-facing IT work, and warm referrals
    convert better than cold messages.
  </p>

